1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,920
If your Apple Watch just flagged a low heart rate, or you've been feeling unusually tired and lightheaded, don't brush it off.

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:10,080
A resting heart rate under 60 beats per minute is called bradycardia.

3
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,640
Not all bradycardia is dangerous.

4
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:24,400
If you exercise regularly, your heart pumps more blood with every beat, so a resting rate in the 40s or 50s can simply mean your heart is working efficiently - that's actually a sign of good cardiovascular fitness.

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:31,840
The real concern shows up when a slow pulse comes with dizziness, fatigue, or fainting, even without an athletic lifestyle.

6
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:38,400
Three everyday factors are worth checking first: sleep apnea, medication, and vagal overstimulation.

7
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,400
Sleep apnea comes first.

8
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:49,280
When your airway repeatedly collapses during sleep, the resulting drop in oxygen triggers an overactive vagal response that pulls your heart rate down.

9
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,760
Loud snoring or waking up exhausted even after a full night's sleep are signs worth paying attention to.

10
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:02,480
Sleeping on your side instead of your back keeps your airway more open, and losing excess weight reduces pressure on it.

11
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:06,160
If snoring is severe, ask your doctor about a sleep study.

12
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:07,760
Medication is second.

13
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:19,840
Beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers, commonly prescribed for high blood pressure or arrhythmias, are designed to slow your heart down - and if the dose isn't quite right for your body, that effect can go further than intended.

14
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:22,640
Never stop a prescribed medication on your own.

15
00:01:22,960 --> 00:01:27,280
If you notice your pulse has dropped noticeably, tell your doctor so the dose can be reviewed.

16
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:29,840
Third is vagal overstimulation.

17
00:01:30,160 --> 00:01:42,000
Straining hard during a bowel movement, splashing your face with cold water, or standing still in one place for a long time, like waiting in a long line, can trigger a strong vagal reflex that drops your heart rate suddenly.

18
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:51,760
Getting enough fiber and water makes straining less necessary, and if you have to stand still for a while, shifting your weight or flexing your calves keeps blood from pooling in your legs.

19
00:01:52,080 --> 00:02:00,240
Managing sleep apnea, checking in with your doctor about medication, and easing off habits that overstimulate the vagus nerve all help stabilize your pulse.

20
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:11,280
An underactive thyroid is another common cause of a slow heart rate, so if you're also feeling unusually cold or gaining weight, ask your doctor to check your thyroid levels at your next checkup.

21
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:18,640
That said, if your heart rate drops below 40 or you experience repeated dizziness or fainting, seek medical care right away.
